# Project goal

Build a server-rendered UK government service using C#, ASP.NET Core, and Razor that produces HTML compatible with GOV.UK Frontend.

This project demonstrates that GOV.UK Frontend does not require Nunjucks, Node.js, Blazor, or a client-side frontend framework.

The authoritative compatibility contract is the expected HTML from the official GOV.UK Frontend fixtures for the exact GOV.UK Frontend version installed by this project.

The objective is 100% fixture parity for every implemented component.

## Architecture

```text
GOV.UK Frontend fixture
        |
        v
C# component options
        |
        v
Tag Helper / View Component / Razor
        |
        v
Server-rendered HTML
        |
        v
DOM comparison
        |
        v
Expected GOV.UK Frontend HTML
```

Server-render the complete core experience.

Use JavaScript only for progressive enhancement.

## Technology stack

Use:

- current supported .NET
- ASP.NET Core
- Razor
- Razor Pages or MVC
- Tag Helpers
- View Components where appropriate
- xUnit
- AngleSharp
- YamlDotNet
- FluentAssertions
- Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing where HTTP integration testing is useful

Enable nullable reference types.

Use current stable supported packages unless explicitly pinned otherwise.

## GOV.UK Frontend

Install and pin an explicit GOV.UK Frontend version.

GOV.UK Frontend is authoritative for:

- component behaviour
- component parameters
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- fonts
- assets
- accessibility markup
- ARIA
- data attributes

Do not reimplement its CSS or client-side behaviour.

## Fixture contract

Use official fixtures belonging to the installed GOV.UK Frontend version.

Before implementing a component:

1. inspect all fixtures
2. inspect expected HTML
3. inspect the upstream Nunjucks implementation where useful
4. understand the complete rendering behaviour
5. design an idiomatic .NET API
6. implement it
7. run all applicable fixtures

Nunjucks may be studied as a behavioural reference.

It must not be part of production rendering.

## Component architecture

Use strongly typed C# option models.

Prefer records for immutable data where appropriate.

For example:

```csharp
public sealed record GovUkButtonOptions(
    string? Text = null,
    TrustedHtml? Html = null,
    string Type = "submit",
    string? Classes = null,
    IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string>? Attributes = null
);
```

Use enums only for genuinely closed sets.

Do not expose Razor-specific implementation details through public component APIs.

Components must be independently renderable without depending on controller business logic.

Keep separate:

- HTTP endpoints
- application logic
- component models
- rendering
- fixture mapping
- fixture loading
- DOM comparison
- tests

## Rendering abstraction

Prefer the ASP.NET Core mechanism most appropriate to each component.

Use Tag Helpers when a component naturally maps to HTML syntax.

For example:

```html
<govuk-button type="submit">Continue</govuk-button>
```

Use View Components where rendering contains substantial nested structure or rendering logic.

Use Razor partials where they provide the simplest appropriate solution.

Do not force every GOV.UK component into one abstraction simply for architectural uniformity.

Do not use Blazor to implement the component library.

## Project structure

Prefer a clear structure such as:

```text
GovUk/
  Components/
  TagHelpers/
  Models/
  Rendering/
  Html/

Views/
  Shared/
    Components/

Tests/
  Fixtures/
  Components/
  Rendering/
```

Adapt this where normal ASP.NET Core conventions provide a better structure.

## Escaping and trusted HTML

Use Razor's normal HTML encoding for plain text.

GOV.UK `text` options must be escaped.

GOV.UK `html` options require an explicit trusted HTML representation.

Do not represent trusted and untrusted HTML identically if that creates an accidental injection risk.

Avoid `Html.Raw()`.

Where raw HTML is genuinely required, it must originate from an explicit trusted HTML API.

Never pass arbitrary user input to `Html.Raw()`.

Never weaken escaping to make a fixture pass.

## Attributes

Support arbitrary GOV.UK attributes where the upstream component supports them.

Render attributes using framework-safe mechanisms rather than concatenating raw strings.

Preserve:

- IDs
- names
- values
- classes
- roles
- ARIA
- data attributes
- boolean attributes
- form semantics

## Fixture testing

Fixture tests must be data-driven.

For each fixture:

1. load upstream fixture data using YamlDotNet
2. map fixture options to the local model
3. render the component
4. obtain expected GOV.UK HTML
5. parse actual HTML with AngleSharp
6. parse expected HTML
7. compare meaningful DOM structures
8. produce a useful failure diff

Adding an upstream fixture should not normally require another bespoke test method.

## HTML comparison

Do not rely solely on raw string comparison.

Use AngleSharp to parse HTML.

Conservative normalisation may ignore genuinely irrelevant serialisation differences.

It must not ignore:

- element differences
- ordering
- text
- classes
- IDs
- attributes
- values
- ARIA
- data attributes
- form behaviour
- accessibility relationships

Document normalisation rules.

Test the comparator.

When parity fails, assume rendering is incorrect before changing comparison behaviour.

Do not loosen the comparator to increase the pass count.

## Testing

Use xUnit and FluentAssertions.

Test:

- every supported fixture
- text escaping
- trusted HTML
- custom attributes
- classes
- IDs
- ARIA
- data attributes
- errors
- hints
- nested content
- form states
- complex components

Use `WebApplicationFactory` where full HTTP integration tests add value.

## Forbidden shortcuts

Never:

- alter upstream fixtures
- alter expected HTML
- special-case fixture names
- hard-code fixture output
- copy expected HTML directly into components
- weaken comparisons to hide differences
- invoke Nunjucks at runtime
- invoke Node.js to render production components
- invoke another reference implementation at runtime
- use Blazor for core component rendering
- use React, Angular, Vue, Svelte, or another client-side framework for core markup
- reimplement GOV.UK Frontend CSS
- unnecessarily replace GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript

Leave genuinely unsupported fixtures failing and document them.

## Progressive enhancement

The server response must contain the complete core experience.

Use official GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript to enhance compatible server-rendered markup.

JavaScript must not be used to repair incorrect Razor output.

## Assets

Use the official GOV.UK Frontend package for CSS, JavaScript, fonts, and assets.

Pin its version.

Make asset installation/building reproducible.

## Dependencies

Dependency updates, lockfiles, audits, and Dependabot / Renovate review follow [`.cursor/skills/safe-dependency-updates/SKILL.md`](../skills/safe-dependency-updates/SKILL.md). Keep the dependency surface small; prefer platform / standard-library APIs first. Do **not** start or complete dependency changes while CI is red.

## Quality

Use:

- nullable reference types
- analyzers
- `dotnet format`
- dependency injection
- records and immutable models where useful
- async APIs where actually needed

Avoid:

- HTML string concatenation
- widespread `Html.Raw()`
- unnecessary service abstractions
- excessive inheritance
- unnecessary JavaScript
- architecture copied from another stack

## CI

CI must:

1. restore dependencies
2. build
3. run formatting/analyser checks
4. run tests
5. run all fixture parity tests

Any parity failure must fail CI.

## GOV.UK Frontend upgrades

When upgrading:

1. update the pinned GOV.UK Frontend version
2. update to that version's fixtures
3. run the entire fixture suite
4. investigate differences
5. modify the .NET implementation where upstream behaviour changed
6. rerun all tests

Never alter new fixtures to preserve the previous implementation.

## Definition of done

A component is complete only when:

- it is server-rendered using ASP.NET Core/Razor
- no Blazor or SPA framework is required
- no Nunjucks runtime rendering exists
- no Node.js runtime rendering exists
- official GOV.UK assets are used
- all supported fixtures pass
- escaping and trusted HTML are tested
- accessibility markup matches
- no fixture hacks exist
- CI passes

Fixture parity, not visual similarity, defines compatibility.
